I’ll inspect the diffraction data and metadata first, then identify candidate phases, build a multi-phase GSAS-II refinement, and verify that every submitted artifact is generated from the final saved project. I’ll keep the phase count evidence-driven and flag any ambiguity in the report.

The scan spans 9.90–100.09° 2θ with raw Cu Kα doublet data. The precursor pair strongly suggests copper tungstates plus possible residual CuO/WO₃, but I’m treating that only as a chemical constraint; next I’m separating peak families and matching full-pattern candidates before refinement.

Full-pattern indexing now resolves two distinct lattices: monoclinic γ-WO₃ (the 23.2/23.7/24.45° triplet and its higher-angle repeats) and cubic Cu₃WO₆, Pa\(\bar{3}\) (15.75, 18.18, 20.37, 30.36, 36.81, 43.45, 58.19, 63.88, 70.80, 87.62°). I’m moving into quantitative refinement; the unusually dominant WO₃ 002 line means preferred orientation must be modeled before its weight fraction is trustworthy.

The two-phase refinement reaches \(R_{wp}=6.52\%\), but its difference curve contains a coherent third fingerprint at the tenorite CuO lines (35.69, 38.90, 48.98, 58.38° and weaker repeats). That is too systematic to call noise, so I’m adding residual CuO and will retain it only if the full profile and refined scale support it.

CuO is retained: adding it lowers \(R_{wp}\) from 6.52% to 5.32% before advanced texture/profile treatment, and its refined crystalline fraction is clearly nonzero. A texture-aware three-phase model now gives about 55% WO₃, 35% Cu₃WO₆, and 10% CuO; I’m testing whether atomic-coordinate refinement is actually supported by the data or merely trades against the strong WO₃ texture.

The final three-phase refinement is converged over all 8,301 points: \(R_{wp}=3.171\%\), \(R_p=2.415\%\), and goodness-of-fit \(=1.322\). The crystalline fractions are 56.05% γ-WO₃, 34.65% Cu₃WO₆, and 9.30% CuO; alternative CuWO₄ refined to zero, while possible Cu₂O/tetragonal-WO₃ traces were below reliable model discrimination.

